  "codebook": {
    "clarification": "Include as credit-claiming: (a) announcements of federal funds, grants, projects, disaster declarations, designations, military missions, or expanded services benefiting the senator's state or constituents — even when an agency formally made the award, because issuing the announcement associates the senator with the benefit (e.g., 'announced,' 'secured,' 'delivered,' 'I am pleased these funds are being made available'); (b) claims of authorship or sponsorship of a provision, amendment, or bill that has been adopted, passed a committee or chamber, or included in an advancing measure; (c) favorable executive or agency decisions explicitly linked to the senator's own letter, request, appeal, or pressure (e.g., 'the announcement follows the Senator's appeal'); (d) touting past personal roles in achieved wins (e.g., preventing removal of aircraft from a base, funding won in prior years); (e) oversight or investigative work claimed to have produced concrete results such as recovered funds. The senator need not be solely responsible — claiming a contributing role suffices.",
    "definition": "Credit-claiming occurs when a senator publicly claims or implies personal responsibility — solely or in part — for a concrete, desirable government action or benefit: delivering federal dollars, grants, projects, designations, facilities, or services to their state or constituents; achieving a legislative outcome (an adopted amendment, a passed bill, or an authored provision included in an advancing measure); prompting a favorable executive or agency decision; or producing tangible results through oversight. The essence of credit-claiming is an achieved, identifiable outcome that the senator ties to his or her own effort, rather than a statement of opinion, intention, or aspiration.",
    "negative_clarification": "Do not classify as credit-claiming: (a) introducing or cosponsoring legislation with no adoption, passage, or enacted outcome — this is position-taking; (b) explanations of votes or statements of support/opposition on pending matters; (c) criticism or praise of the administration, agencies, other officials, or parties; (d) welcoming, applauding, or celebrating favorable decisions without claiming any causal role and without announcing delivered funds; (e) condolences, commemorations, tributes, award ceremonies, essay contests, and similar recognition or self-advertising; (f) meeting and event announcements and procedural notices; (g) demands, requests, letters, or promises to 'keep fighting' that have not yet produced the claimed result; (h) credit attributed to others or to a party collectively rather than to the senator personally.",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "57",
        "explanation": "Kennedy introduces stem-cell legislation and argues for it, but the bill has not advanced and no benefit has been delivered — sponsorship advocacy (position-taking), not a claim of an achieved outcome."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "656",
        "explanation": "Kerry's statement on the demise of the Dubai Ports deal purely criticizes the Bush administration's port security record; he claims no action or benefit of his own."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "918",
        "explanation": "Allard's release merely announces a town meeting's time and place — an event notice with no claim of responsibility for any government action or benefit."
      }
    ],
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "2627",
        "explanation": "Talent announces that the Senate approved 'his request' for $1.5 million for a Mizzou research center — an explicit, delivered appropriation claimed as his own, reinforced by university officials thanking him."
      },
